,{"season":2018,"team":"BUF","distance":0.7187,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"WAS","distance":0.7376,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"ARI","distance":0.8162,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"SF","distance":0.834,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Panthers went 7-10 in 2022 and were outscored 374 to 347. The offense ranked 20th in EPA per play and the defense 16th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the highest singleback usage and the 2nd highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","offense":"The Panthers offense was a little below average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the highest singleback usage and the lowest empty formation usage in the league. Baker Mayfield ran the passing game and leaned heavily on D.J. Moore, who drew 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Panthers defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Even its strongest area, EPA allowed per rush, only ranked 13th, and it was 26th in takeaways per game. Brian Burns was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Frankie Luvu, who had 7 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","special":"The Panthers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 94% of field goals, which was the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 9% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 83% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Baker Mayfield played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,313 yards. D.J. Moore was the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 888 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by D'Onta Foreman and Chuba Hubbard. On defense, Brian Burns led the team with 12.5 sacks and Jaycee Horn picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Lions under Ben Johnson and the 2023 Titans. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Lions under Aaron Glenn and the 2018 Bills.","trends":"The offense went from 30th in EPA per play in 2021 to 20th in 2022, a big step forward. The defense moved from 7th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 16th in 2022, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 1 rate, which went from 29% to 17%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}